          "coreNotes": "\n            <div class=\"space-y-4 text-slate-200\">\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-amber-400\">💎 1. Elements, Minerals, and Rocks</h4>\n                <p class=\"text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed\">\n                  • <strong>Element:</strong> A pure chemical substance consisting of only one kind of atom (e.g. oxygen, silicon, iron).<br>\n                  • <strong>Mineral:</strong> A naturally occurring, inorganic solid with a characteristic chemical composition and an orderly crystalline atomic structure.<br>\n                  • <strong>Rock:</strong> A naturally occurring aggregate of one or more minerals or mineral-like materials.\n                </p>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-rose-400\">🪨 2. The Three Great Rock Families</h4>\n                <div class=\"space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300\">\n                  <p>• <strong>1. Igneous Rocks:</strong> Formed when molten rock (magma or lava) cools and solidifies.<br>\n                  - <em>Intrusive igneous rocks:</em> Cool slowly <strong>below the surface</strong>, forming coarse, large crystals (e.g., granite).<br>\n                  - <em>Extrusive igneous rocks:</em> Cool rapidly <strong>at the surface</strong> after eruption, forming fine-grained or glassy textures (e.g., basalt, obsidian, pumice).</p>\n                  <p>• <strong>2. Sedimentary Rocks:</strong> Formed from weathered rock fragments deposited as sediments that undergo compaction and cementation (lithification), or from chemical and biological accumulation (e.g., sandstone, limestone, shale).</p>\n                  <p>• <strong>3. Metamorphic Rocks:</strong> Formed when existing rocks are altered by intense heat, confining pressure, and chemically active fluids without completely melting (e.g., marble from limestone, slate from shale, quartzite from sandstone).</p>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          ",
