          "coreNotes": "\n            <div class=\"space-y-4 text-slate-200\">\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-amber-400\">🌊 1. River Valley Civilizations</h4>\n                <div class=\"space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300\">\n                  <p>• <strong>Mesopotamia:</strong> Situated between the <strong>Tigris and Euphrates rivers</strong> (the \"Cradle of Civilization\"). Sumerian civilization invented <strong>cuneiform</strong> (earliest wedge-shaped writing system), the wheel, the 60-minute time division, and the Code of Hammurabi.<br>\n                  • <strong>Ancient Egypt:</strong> Flourished along the fertile floodplains of the <strong>Nile River</strong>. Developed centralized pharaonic government, monumental pyramid tombs, hieroglyphic writing, geometry, and a 365-day solar calendar.<br>\n                  • <strong>Indus Valley Civilization:</strong> Developed along the <strong>Indus River system</strong> (Harappa and Mohenjo-daro). Renowned for sophisticated urban grid planning, baked brick houses, and covered underground drainage networks.<br>\n                  • <strong>Ancient China:</strong> Emerged along the <strong>Yellow (Huang He) and Yangtze rivers</strong>. Contributed paper, silk, printing, compass, gunpowder, and the ethical philosophy of Confucianism.</p>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400\">🏛️ 2. Classical Antiquity: Greece and Rome</h4>\n                <div class=\"space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300\">\n                  <p>• <strong>Ancient Greece:</strong> City-state (Polis) system. <strong>Athens</strong> developed the world's first system of <strong>direct democracy</strong> for free citizens. Major contributions in philosophy (Socrates, Plato, Aristotle), mathematics (Pythagoras, Euclid), literature, science, and the Olympic Games.<br>\n                  • <strong>Ancient Rome:</strong> Transitioned from a Republic into a vast Empire spanning the Mediterranean. Mastered civil engineering (aqueducts, stone arches, vaulted colosseums, 80,000 km paved road network) and established the principles of <strong>Roman Law</strong> (innocent until proven guilty, equality under law).</p>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          ",
